<chapter id="funsetmap">
#title("Linear Sets and Maps")

#para("\

Both (finite) sets and (finite) maps are commonly used data structures.
Functional sets and maps are immutable after their construction.
Insertion into or removal from a functional set/map results in the
construction of a new set/map while the original is kept intact, and the
newly constructed set/map and the original one share a lot of underlying
representation. Such sharing is incompatible with explicit freeing: a
functional set/map can only be reclaimed through garbage collection (GC).
The functional sets and maps of ATSLIB (the ones in
#filename("libats/ML")) were removed together with the support for GC.

")

#para("\

What ATSLIB provides is #emphasis("linear") sets and maps: an insertion
into or a removal from a linear set/map updates it in place (it is passed by
reference), and the set/map has to be freed by the programmer, which the
type-checker enforces. In this chapter, I show how a linear set and a linear
map based on balanced trees (AVL trees) can be created and operated on.

")

<sect1
 id="funsetmap_set">
#title("Linear Sets")

#para('\

Suppose that a set is needed for collecting values of type
#stacode("elt"). The following code sets up the interface and the
implementation of a set based on the AVL-tree implementation in
ATSLIB/libats (the implementation of the sets needs the implementation of
the queues of lists, too). Note that a comparison function on values of the
type #stacode("elt") is needed, which is implemented here with the overloaded
symbol #dyncode("compare"). If this symbol is not overloaded for the
type, one needs to implement such a function.

#dats2xhtml('\
staload "libats/SATS/linset_avltree.sats"
//
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/qlist.dats"
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/linset_avltree.dats"
//
typedef elt = int
//
implement
compare_elt_elt<elt>(x, y) = compare(x, y)
')

')

#para("\

Assume that #stacode("elt") is #stacode("int"). The following line
of code creates a linear set (of integers) containing no elements:

#dats2xhtml('\
var myset: set(elt) = linset_make_nil{elt}()
')

The function for inserting an element into a given set is assigned the
following type:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
linset_insert
  (xs: &set(INV(a)) >> _, x0: a):<!wrt> bool
')

Note that the first argument of #dyncode("linset_insert") is
call-by-reference, which is why #dyncode("myset") is declared as a
variable (with the keyword #keycode("var")). If the given element is
inserted into the given set, then the set is updated and #dyncode("false")
is returned (to indicate no error).  Otherwise, #dyncode("true") is
returned (to indicate that the element is already in the set). The
following few lines of code shows how insertion can be operated on a set:

#dats2xhtml('\
val-false = linset_insert(myset, 0) // inserted
val-(true) = linset_insert(myset, 0) // not actually inserted
val-false = linset_insert(myset, 1) // inserted
val-(true) = linset_insert(myset, 1) // not actually inserted
')

During the course of debugging, one may want to print out the values
contained in a given set:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = assertloc (linset_size(myset) = 2)
val () = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "myset = ", myset)
')

where the symbol #dyncode("fprint") is overloaded with
#dyncode("fprint_linset"). The function for removing an element from a
given set is assigned the following type:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
linset_remove
  (xs: &set(INV(a)) >> _, x0: a):<!wrt> bool
')

If the given element is removed from the given set, then the set is
updated and #dyncode("true") is returned.  Otherwise,
#dyncode("false") is returned.  The following few lines of code shows how
removal can be operated on a set:

#dats2xhtml('\
val-true = linset_remove(myset, 0) // removed
val-false = linset_remove(myset, 0) // not actually removed
val-true = linset_remove(myset, 1) // removed
val-false = linset_remove(myset, 1) // not actually removed
')

")

#para('\

A set has to be consumed before the end of its scope. The function
#dyncode("linset_free") frees a set, and the function
#dyncode("linset_listize") turns it into a linear list of the elements
(in increasing order) and, in the process, consumes the set. The
function #dyncode("linset_foreach") visits the elements of a set that is
only borrowed:

#dats2xhtml('\
val-false = linset_insert(myset, 3)
val-false = linset_insert(myset, 1)
val-false = linset_insert(myset, 2)
//
val () = let
  implement
  linset_foreach$fwork<elt><void>(x, env) = println! ("x = ", x)
in
  linset_foreach<elt>(myset)
end // end of [val]
//
val xs = linset_listize(myset) // [myset] is consumed
val () = println! ("xs = ", xs)
val () = list_vt_free<elt>(xs)
')

')#comment("para")

#para('\

Various common set operations can be found in
#myatscodelink("libats/SATS/SHARE/linset.hats",
"libats/SATS/SHARE/linset.hats").  By following the types assigned to these
operations, one should have no difficulty in figuring out how they are
supposed to be called.  Please find the entirety of the code used in this
section #mycodelink("chap_funsetmap_set.dats", "on-line").

')

</sect1><!--"funsetmap_set"-->

<sect1
 id="funsetmap_map">
#title("Linear Maps")

#para('\

Suppose that a map is needed for mapping keys of type
#stacode("key") to items of type #stacode("itm"). The following code
sets up the interface and the implementation of a map based on the
AVL-tree implementation in ATSLIB/libats. As for sets, a comparison
function on keys is needed:

#dats2xhtml('\
staload "libats/SATS/linmap_avltree.sats"
//
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/qlist.dats"
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/linmap_avltree.dats"
//
typedef key = string and itm = int
//
implement
compare_key_key<key>(x, y) = compare(x, y)
')

')

#para("\

Assume that #stacode("key") is #stacode("string") and #stacode("itm")
is #stacode("int"). The following line of code creates an empty linear map:

#dats2xhtml('\
var mymap: map(key, itm) = linmap_make_nil{key,itm}()
')

The following few lines insert some key/item pairs into
#dyncode("mymap"):

#dats2xhtml('\
val-~None_vt() = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "a", 0)
val-~Some_vt(0) = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "a", 1)
//
val-~None_vt() = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "b", 1)
val-~Some_vt(1) = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "b", 2)
//
val-~None_vt() = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "c", 2)
val-~Some_vt(2) = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "c", 3)
')

Given a key and an item, #dyncode("linmap_insert_opt") inserts the key/item
pair into #dyncode("mymap") (it is passed by reference, hence the
variable).  If the key is in the domain of the map represented by
#dyncode("mymap") before insertion, then the original item associated with
the key is returned (in the linear option-value built from
#dyncode("Some_vt")). Otherwise, #dyncode("None_vt") is returned.
The patterns #dyncode("val-~None_vt()") and #dyncode("val-~Some_vt(2)")
free the returned option while they match it. As can be expected,
the size of #dyncode("mymap") is 3 at this point:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = assertloc (linmap_size(mymap) = 3)
val () = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "mymap = ", mymap)
')

The function #dyncode("linmap_size") returns the number of key/item pairs
stored in a given map (in constant time).  During the course of debugging,
one may want to print out the key/item pairs in a given map; the symbol
#dyncode("fprint") is overloaded with #dyncode("fprint_linmap"). The next
two lines of code show how search with a given key operates on a map:

#dats2xhtml('\
val-~None_vt() = linmap_search_opt(mymap, "")
val-~Some_vt(1) = linmap_search_opt(mymap, "a")
')

The function #dyncode("linmap_search_opt") returns the item associated with
a given key if it is found. The next few lines of code remove some key/item
pairs from #dyncode("mymap"):

#dats2xhtml('\
val-true = linmap_remove(mymap, "a")
val-false = linmap_remove(mymap, "a")
//
val-~Some_vt(2) = linmap_takeout_opt(mymap, "b")
val-~Some_vt(3) = linmap_takeout_opt(mymap, "c")
')

The function #dyncode("linmap_remove") removes the key/item pair of a given
key. If a key/item pair is removed, then the function returns
true. Otherwise, it returns false to indicates that no key/item pair of the
given key is stored in the map being operated on.  The function
#dyncode("linmap_takeout_opt") is similar to #dyncode("linmap_remove")
excepting for returning the removed item.

")

#para('\

The key/item pairs of a map can be visited with the template
#dyncode("linmap_foreach") (there is no closure-function to create: the
template #dyncode("linmap_foreach$fwork") is implemented to specify the
work to do for each pair). Finally, the map is freed with
#dyncode("linmap_free"):

#dats2xhtml('\
val-~None_vt() = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "x", 10)
val-~None_vt() = linmap_insert_opt(mymap, "y", 20)
//
val () = let
  implement
  linmap_foreach$fwork<key,itm><void>
    (k, x, env) = println! ("k=", k, " and ", "x=", x)
in
  linmap_foreach<key,itm>(mymap)
end // end of [val]
//
val () = linmap_free(mymap)
')

')#comment("para")

#para('\

Various common map operations can be found in
#myatscodelink("libats/SATS/SHARE/linmap.hats",
"libats/SATS/SHARE/linmap.hats").  By following the types assigned to these
operations, one should have no difficulty in figuring out how they are
supposed to be called. Please find the entirety of the code used in this
section #mycodelink("chap_funsetmap_map.dats", "on-line").

')#comment("para")

</sect1><!--"funsetmap_map"-->

</chapter><!--"funsetmap"-->

